1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,900
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome to this series on orthodontics.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:11,300
 So this is my personal favorite specialty since I'm currently studying orthodontics

3
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:12,740
 as a resident.

4
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:15,580
 We're going to cover a ton of great content.

5
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:21,300
 The board examiners combine orthodontics and pediatric dentistry into one category in

6
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:26,720
 their guidelines, but I think it's honestly more helpful to focus on each one individually.

7
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:32,520
 I've already made the Pediatric Dentistry Series, if you want to go check that out.

8
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:37,320
 So that being said, like all of my videos, I'm going to focus on the highest yield things

9
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:42,420
 that you need to know for the exam and my hope is that these videos will help you prepare

10
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:49,780
 for the board exam and also give you an overview for clinical application and general knowledge.

11
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:54,080
 So we're going to start by talking about growth and development and I like to think of growth

12
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,680
 and development as two sides of the same coin.

13
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:03,820
 They are two distinctly different things, but they work in harmony towards the same goal.

14
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:08,680
 So growth is all about an increase in size or number.

15
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:12,520
 An increase in size of cells is called hypertrophy.

16
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:16,040
 An increase in number of cells is called hyperplasia.

17
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:21,880
 And growth has to do with anatomic things, height, weight, length, width.

18
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:24,600
 They're all quantitative numbers.

19
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:31,120
 Now development is an increase in complexity or specialization of functions, and this is

20
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,480
 more physiological or behavioral.

21
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:40,440
 So it's more qualitative things, day-to-day function, how you learn, how you act and behave,

22
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,000
 and things like that.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,720
 Growth and development follow three basic laws.

24
00:01:45,720 --> 00:01:51,040
 There's a pattern, so their predictable changes in how a body will grow over time.

25
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:56,580
 There's general timing, ages where we expect growth and ages where we don't, but there's

26
00:01:56,580 --> 00:02:02,960
 also some variability in how an individual person will grow and develop with time.

27
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:07,880
 So there's a pattern, timing, and variability.

28
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:12,680
 One such pattern is the cephalocaudal growth gradient.

29
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:20,080
 So this is saying that body parts closer to the cranium grow fast early on while body

30
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:25,640
 parts further from the cranium grow more later on.

31
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:31,280
 So another way to think of it is the brain is the trailblazer and everything else has

32
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,400
 to catch up later on.

33
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:37,400
 So we can see some examples of this phenomenon in these pictures.

34
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:45,520
 So two months in utero, this baby has a head that comprises 50% of its total length, but

35
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:50,840
 once we get to three to four months in utero, now the head only comprises 30%.

36
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:58,160
 At birth, the head is 25%, and then as an adult, the head is about 12% of the total height.

37
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:03,560
 You can also look at the upper limbs versus the lower limbs, so the upper limbs grow more

38
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:09,240
 earlier, and then the lower limbs have to catch up later on in life.

39
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:12,040
 The same can be said for the maxilla and the mandible.

40
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:19,820
 The maxilla will fully mature earlier than the mandible does because the maxilla is closer

41
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:24,320
 to the cranium.

42
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:30,720
 So one visual depiction of a typical growth pattern and timing of that pattern is the

43
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:33,680
 scammon's growth curves.

44
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:41,760
 So these growth curves show age on the x-axis in years and tissue size on the y-axis.

45
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:48,040
 So from top to bottom we have the lymphoid growth curve, so that's your immune tissues,

46
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:53,920
 the neural growth curve, so that's like your brain, then we have maxilla, mandible, general

47
00:03:53,920 --> 00:04:00,320
 body tissues like bones and muscles, and then finally genital tissues on the bottom.

48
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:07,600
 So each of these has a unique pattern to it, and neural tissues, including the brain, grow

49
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:15,160
 rapidly after birth, the steepest of these curves, and then reach near adult size by

50
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:20,680
 about age 6 or 7, and then kind of levels out.

51
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:28,720
 Lymphoid tissues also grow rapidly, reaching twice their adult size by age 10, so note

52
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:34,860
 that this is a percent of adult size, and so we're actually surpassing what the lymphoid

53
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:37,120
 tissue mass will be as an adult.

54
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:43,140
 So we're way up here at about 10 years, and then they involute or shrink during the pubertal

55
00:04:43,140 --> 00:04:51,200
 growth spurt to reach their final growth adult size at 100%.

56
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:57,080
 The genital tissues do not grow much at all until you hit the pubertal growth spurt, and

57
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:03,040
 then they rapidly increase to that 100% adult size.

58
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:08,640
 The general body tissues grow rapidly after birth, they kind of slow down in childhood,

59
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:14,040
 and then grow rapidly again during the pubertal growth spurt.

60
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:19,400
 And then finally we have the maxilla and mandible, and so the maxilla follows a bit closer to

61
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:25,360
 the neural growth curve, because it's closer to the brain, and the mandible follows more

62
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:30,840
 closer to the general growth curve, because it's further away from the brain.

63
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:40,160
 So we see that cephalocaudal growth gradient manifesting in these really neat growth curves.

64
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:48,720
 So notice how the lymphoid and genital curves after age 10, so if we mark off here and here,

65
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:54,160
 they kind of act opposite to each other, they're like mirror images, the lymphoid goes way

66
00:05:54,160 --> 00:06:00,240
 down, and the genital goes way up during that growth spurt, and that's because as the concentration

67
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:06,200
 of sex hormones estrogen and testosterone increases, they drive the growth spurt, and

68
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:11,520
 this inhibits the further growth of lymphoid tissue, which actually begins to shrink, eventually

69
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:15,200
 reaching that normal adult size.

70
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:21,120
 So Scammons is one way, and we also have distant and velocity human growth curves to represent

71
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:22,120
 growth over time.

72
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:25,560
 So let's look at the distance curve first.

73
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:30,680
 Now pediatricians often keep track of their patient's growth with charts.

74
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:34,760
 The patient will stand against the wall, mark how tall they are, and that's your distance

75
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:36,960
 curve in black.

76
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:42,760
 It's showing how much size you've accumulated over time.

77
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:46,120
 So that's never really going to go down, it's only going to increase.

78
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:51,800
 The velocity curve in gray shows the rate of growth.

79
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:57,040
 So if you remember in physics, it's showing the slope of the distance curve.

80
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:03,720
 So if you have a big increase in a high slope in the beginning, that high slope is going

81
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:08,160
 to be indicated by the velocity curve being way up here.

82
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:15,240
 And then as that growth starts to taper off, our velocity curve shows that as well.

83
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:23,120
 And then once we get to that growth spurt at around 12, 13, 14, we can see an acceleration

84
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:29,560
 in growth and how the velocity increases to show that what's happening on the distance

85
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:30,560
 curve.

86
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:36,320
 So the distance curve tracks actual height each year while the velocity curve tracks the

87
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:46,440
 change in height which allows you to more easily observe growth velocity like the growth spurt.

88
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:51,400
 So these are specific velocity curves for boys and girls.

89
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:59,120
 And it shows that girls hit their growth spurt earlier and mature earlier than boys do in

90
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,320
 general.

91
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:04,800
 And boys mature more and later on in their lifetime.

92
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:09,280
 So the average peak growth for girls is at around age 12.

93
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:15,160
 That peak growth time for boys is at around age 14 a little bit later.

94
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:21,760
 Generally, the earlier your peak growth is, that means the shorter duration that growth

95
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:27,120
 spurt will be and the less overall growth that you will see.

96
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:32,480
 So we looked at some growth patterns and we talked about some of the things that happen

97
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:36,920
 with specific tissues and humans and boys and girls.

98
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:40,440
 Now let's talk more about timing of growth.

99
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:44,920
 So there are a number of different things you can go off of to determine how mature someone

100
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:45,920
 is.

101
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:51,520
 The easiest thing would be to go up their chronologic age because everybody knows their age.

102
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:58,040
 But that's often not a good indicator of maturity because there's variability.

103
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:01,360
 Some people mature early, some people mature late.

104
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:07,040
 So we have other things to help us judge just how mature that person is.

105
00:09:07,040 --> 00:09:09,400
 One of those things is dental age.

106
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:15,600
 And this is based on their state of dental development, how many primary teeth they have,

107
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:20,600
 how many primary teeth have exfoliated, how many permanent teeth have come in.

108
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:25,560
 But this is unfortunately a pretty poor indicator of maturity.

109
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:31,400
 The next thing we have is skeletal age, and this can be measured by two methods.

110
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:39,160
 One of them is the CVM staging, which is the cervical vertebral maturation.

111
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:46,560
 So you take a cephalometric x-ray, you look at how their cervical vertebrae are shaped

112
00:09:46,560 --> 00:09:51,120
 on that cephalometric, and that gives you an indication of how mature that individual

113
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:52,840
 might be.

114
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:58,440
 The other popular method is not as popular in any more, it is called the hand-risk method,

115
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:04,680
 where you take an actual x-ray of that person's hand and you can look at the finger bones

116
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:09,360
 and the wrist bones and see how those are developing to determine someone's maturity.

117
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:13,920
 This is a better indicator of maturity, but not the best.

118
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:21,160
 So biologic or developmental age is based on certain markers of maturation, like we can

119
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:28,280
 see here, menarchy or first menstruation, which occurs just after the peak velocity

120
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:29,280
 of growth.

121
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:35,880
 Also, you can go off secondary sex characteristics like facial hair, and this is the best indicator

122
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:40,080
 of an individual's growth status and maturity.

123
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:43,960
 So we kind of go from worst to best in this list.

124
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:49,880
 All right, so now let's talk about how does growth actually happen.

125
00:10:49,880 --> 00:10:54,400
 So we have growth sites, and we have growth centers.

126
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:59,280
 So a site is any area where growth is occurring.

127
00:10:59,280 --> 00:11:07,040
 A center is a site of growth, so growth is occurring also at centers, but they also have

128
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:11,600
 the ability to grow independently on their own.

129
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:16,280
 So growth centers kind of have a mind of their own, and they can control their own growth,

130
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:21,480
 and sometimes even control the growth of other growth sites.

131
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:27,240
 As far as craniofacial structures go, most of the things we're going to be talking about

132
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:28,440
 are sites.

133
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:33,200
 The only true growth center is a synchondrosis.

134
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:39,000
 Sutures, surfaces, and the mandibular condyles are all sites.

135
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,880
 And we'll talk more about this a little bit later.

136
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:48,600
 So there are two main modes of growth.

137
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:55,880
 The first one is endochondrial ossification, which I equate with interstitial growth.

138
00:11:55,880 --> 00:12:04,920
 So this is growth from the inside, and it results in an increase in length of bones.

139
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:12,000
 So we start with an embryonic cartilage model or template that grows first, and is eventually

140
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,520
 replaced by bone.

141
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:19,360
 So we have this zone of resting cartilage.

142
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:25,400
 We're looking at an epiphyseal plate here, and the zone of resting cartilage is at the

143
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:28,400
 very end of this bone.

144
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:35,240
 So we're looking at an epiphysis, and this is the very end of that bone.

145
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:41,120
 So the resting cartilage will have these small little chondrocytes, and they're really hard

146
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:48,560
 to see here, but they're just these little tiny condrocyte cells nestled in cartilage.

147
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:52,280
 Then we have this zone of proliferation.

148
00:12:52,280 --> 00:12:58,800
 And so we're getting hyperplasia, rapid mitotic division, and it results in these columns of

149
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:04,240
 cells that are parallel with the direction of growth.

150
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:08,400
 And this is contributing to that increase in length.

151
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:13,720
 Then we get to the zone of maturation, where we see hypertrophy.

152
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:19,280
 The cells are starting to get bigger, but the walls of the cells are getting thinner.

153
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:26,200
 Then we get to the zone of calcified cartilage, or calcification, where mineral is deposited,

154
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,160
 and the condrocytes are actually destroyed.

155
00:13:28,160 --> 00:13:33,120
 You can note that there are kind of outlines that are missing nuclei.

156
00:13:33,120 --> 00:13:39,280
 So now we have some dead cells, but with the beginnings of calcification and mineralization.

157
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:47,080
 And then finally, we have the zone of ossification, where these empty spaces are invaded by capillaries

158
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:49,680
 that form centers of ossification.

159
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:56,440
 They dump off these progenitor cells that become osteoblasts that can secrete osteoid, which

160
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:59,040
 eventually becomes bone.

161
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:06,480
 So an endochondrosification bone develops from a pre-existing cartilage model, and it's under

162
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:09,720
 more direct genetic control.

163
00:14:09,720 --> 00:14:16,560
 So we see this in epiphyseal plates of long bones, synchondroses of the cranial base,

164
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:22,440
 and also in the condular cartilage of the mandible.

165
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:28,440
 Now the other one here is the intramembranous ossification.

166
00:14:28,440 --> 00:14:33,640
 And this I equate with oppositional growth or mesenchymal growth.

167
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:41,160
 And so this is growth from the outside, and it results in an increase in thickness or diameter.

168
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:47,920
 So now we're looking at the sides of the bone, and as you get more circumferential lamella

169
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:53,160
 laid down along the outside, you get more diameter.

170
00:14:53,160 --> 00:15:00,520
 And at the same time, bone is being resorbed by osteoclasts in the center, from the inside

171
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:02,400
 in the medullary cavity.

172
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:07,600
 So the bone growth stays proportional throughout childhood and adolescence.

173
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:14,000
 So here osteoblasts are secreting their osteoid matrix directly within the connective tissue

174
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:20,240
 lining, and then it calcifies without any intermediate cartilage at all.

175
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:27,280
 So here bone develops from fibrous connective tissue, and it's influenced more from environmental

176
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:29,680
 forces like stresses.

177
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:35,760
 So this is what happens at your sutures or on the surfaces of the cranial vault, the

178
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:39,040
 maxilla, and other craniofacial structures.

179
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:43,040
 So we'll talk more about those specifics in a little bit, but I just wanted to lay the

180
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:50,440
 foundation for endochondral, which you can kind of piece apart to mean within cartilage

181
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:53,360
 and intermembranous within the membrane.

182
00:15:53,360 --> 00:16:00,520
 Those are two big, the two big modes of bone growth.

183
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:07,320
 Now there are three different mechanisms that have been proposed for growth, and no one theory

184
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:10,920
 explains all of craniofacial growth control.

185
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:15,960
 So we kind of have to consider all three of these working in concert.

186
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:19,560
 So Harry Sitcher came up with the suture theory.

187
00:16:19,560 --> 00:16:27,960
 This is where direct genetic control determines how bone is going to grow, and bone will determine

188
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:35,200
 its own growth, and sutures act as growth centers that push stuff apart.

189
00:16:35,200 --> 00:16:42,000
 This one is mostly debunked as false, because we know that the growing brain pushes skull

190
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:46,920
 bones apart, and new bone is laid down at the sutures in response.

191
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:53,240
 So sutures aren't really -- they're more so reactive, they're not really proactive.

192
00:16:53,240 --> 00:16:59,920
 So their growth sites, they're not growth centers, as Harry Sitcher pointed out.

193
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:05,240
 The James Scott came up with the cartilage theory, and this posits that epigenetic growth

194
00:17:05,240 --> 00:17:10,960
 control has cartilage pushing and pulling stuff apart.

195
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:16,760
 So in the cartilage theory, cartilage is the growth center that determines growth,

196
00:17:16,760 --> 00:17:20,160
 and bone simply follows along.

197
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:23,040
 Now this one definitely has some truth to it.

198
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:29,760
 If we look at this example of a condler fracture, condler fractures usually happen on the condler

199
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:35,920
 neck, on the side opposite to the side of impact, and then that disconnected condyle

200
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:45,040
 gets pulled away, it gets absorbed, and then 75% of the time it regenerates, and 25% of

201
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:50,080
 the time it doesn't, leading to growth problems like jaw asymmetry.

202
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:56,320
 But how could it regenerate by itself if it didn't have some kind of intrinsic control?

203
00:17:56,320 --> 00:18:03,520
 So perhaps there is some truth that cartilage has some intrinsic growth potential.

204
00:18:03,520 --> 00:18:09,400
 And then Melvin Moss came up with the functional matrix theory, and this is where this is governed

205
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:11,880
 by environmental growth control.

206
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:18,960
 So chewing, speaking, and other functions cause the nasal and oral cavities to get bigger,

207
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:25,360
 which serve as the primary determinants of growth for the maxilla and the mandible.

208
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:33,320
 So here the soft tissue matrix determines growth, and bone and cartilage follow that.

209
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:37,960
 So there's really good evidence for this, and it's what we use before to disprove the

210
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:44,400
 suture theory, that the growing brain, which is a soft tissue, pushes those skull bones

211
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:50,960
 apart, and the sutures are reacting to that pressure and laying down bone between those

212
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:53,360
 skull bones.

213
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:59,920
 So all three theories, even though suture theory is mostly disproved, work in concert

214
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:07,760
 to describe the different mechanisms by which the craniofacial structures grow.

215
00:19:07,760 --> 00:19:13,440
 So some pretty cool stuff, and here are the five main regions of the cranium in face.

216
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:22,120
 We have the cranial vault, the cranial base, the nasomaxillary complex, the mandible, and

217
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:27,560
 then the two dentoalveolar processes supporting the teeth, and we'll go through how each

218
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:32,920
 of those grows specifically.

219
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:38,880
 So the cranial vault is the top part of the skull that encases the brain.

220
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:47,920
 At birth, the cranial bones are widely separated by this loose connective tissue called fontanels.

221
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:53,440
 So these wide spaces enable delivery of the baby through the birth canal.

222
00:19:53,440 --> 00:20:00,600
 Now, bone is laid down along the membranes of these bones, and that eventually will shrink

223
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:04,040
 down these spaces, at which point they're called sutures.

224
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:10,160
 So sutures are thinner spaces that separate the cranial bones in childhood, adolescence,

225
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:14,200
 and then they eventually fuse later in adult life.

226
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:20,000
 So as the brain grows, these cranial bones are being pushed apart.

227
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:25,800
 And so in order to keep this space from opening wider and wider, new bone is being laid down

228
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:31,560
 along these membranes, and that will occur at these sutures.

229
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:36,680
 So this is an example of intermembranous ossification.

230
00:20:36,680 --> 00:20:42,120
 Intermembranous results in an increase in diameter, so the entire skull is getting wider

231
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:48,440
 and wider, and the bone will be filling in at the fontanels and sutures to keep up with

232
00:20:48,440 --> 00:20:50,400
 the growing brain.

233
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:57,840
 But also, you have direct apposition of bone outside of the skull and resorption or removal

234
00:20:57,840 --> 00:20:59,960
 of bone from the inside.

235
00:20:59,960 --> 00:21:04,080
 So if we go back to this image, you can imagine that in addition to what's happening at the

236
00:21:04,080 --> 00:21:11,200
 sutures, new bone is being laid down at the outside, which we call apposition, and bone

237
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:16,880
 is being removed from the inside, which is called resorption.

238
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:22,080
 So we're getting intermembranous ossification not only at the sutures, but also at the surfaces

239
00:21:22,080 --> 00:21:24,400
 as well.

240
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:31,840
 The cranial base consists of the ethmoid, sphenoid, and occipital bones at the base of

241
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:32,840
 the skull.

242
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:35,720
 Now these three bones are initially cartilage.

243
00:21:35,720 --> 00:21:43,000
 They're part of the chondro cranium in the fetus, and they later transform into bone.

244
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:50,360
 Now we're left, though, with three bands of cartilage that stick around that are important

245
00:21:50,360 --> 00:21:55,640
 growth centers called synchondroses.

246
00:21:55,640 --> 00:22:02,680
 So each synchondrosus acts like a two-sided epiphyseal plate that we saw before, so you

247
00:22:02,680 --> 00:22:10,440
 can imagine that there's this really tiny thin zone of resting cartilage in the center,

248
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:17,400
 and then maturing cartilage is being pushed out in either direction in order to expand

249
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:19,680
 the bone at that zone.

250
00:22:19,680 --> 00:22:26,760
 So these bones are being literally split apart from each other by the synchondrosus, which

251
00:22:26,760 --> 00:22:33,400
 continues to grow and increases the length of this structure.

252
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:38,560
 So this is, of course, an example of endocondrel ossification, because we're dealing with a

253
00:22:38,560 --> 00:22:41,080
 cartilage model.

254
00:22:41,080 --> 00:22:45,000
 The intersphenoid synchondrosus will fuse and close first.

255
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:52,840
 It's about inactive at by age three, sphenoid ethmoid by age seven, and spheno occipital

256
00:22:52,840 --> 00:22:59,000
 is the last one to become inactive.

257
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:00,640
 Next let's talk about the maxilla.

258
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:10,200
 So growth occurs at the sutures, posterior and superior to the nasomexillary complex

259
00:23:10,200 --> 00:23:16,360
 at its connections to the cranial vault and the cranial base.

260
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:25,040
 So this area right here is this cranial base that we looked at right here.

261
00:23:25,040 --> 00:23:29,960
 It's kind of like a mid sagittal slice of the skull, and that's that component right

262
00:23:29,960 --> 00:23:30,960
 there.

263
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:35,400
 The cranial vault is, of course, this part right here.

264
00:23:35,400 --> 00:23:42,880
 And so we're getting a position of bone at these sutures, because soft tissue, that's

265
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:50,440
 the nasal and oral cavities which are expanding, are pulling this maxilla downward and forward

266
00:23:50,440 --> 00:23:53,960
 away from the cranial base.

267
00:23:53,960 --> 00:24:02,640
 Simultaneously, there's also surface remodeling that includes resorption of bone anteriorly,

268
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:10,660
 an opposition of bone inferiorly at the palate, and then also at the alveolar ridges as the

269
00:24:10,660 --> 00:24:12,800
 teeth erupt down.

270
00:24:12,800 --> 00:24:19,880
 There's also addition of bone at the tuberosity posteriorly to make room for the second and

271
00:24:19,880 --> 00:24:22,720
 third molars.

272
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:27,640
 So the ultimate result of all of this is a downward and forward translation of the maxilla

273
00:24:27,640 --> 00:24:31,520
 away from the cranial base.

274
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:34,000
 The mandible has some interesting steps to it.

275
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:42,960
 So it develops in the embryo just lateral to the cartilage of the first pharyngeal arch,

276
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:46,520
 which is called meckles cartilage.

277
00:24:46,520 --> 00:24:51,000
 But the confusing part is that this is not endocondrel growth.

278
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:54,960
 So meckles cartilage isn't being replaced by bone.

279
00:24:54,960 --> 00:25:03,320
 And the mandible, which is here, just happens to be created intermembranously right next

280
00:25:03,320 --> 00:25:05,600
 to it.

281
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:12,800
 So meckles cartilage actually will disintegrate and contribute to the malleus, anchus, and

282
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:15,120
 sphenomandibular ligament.

283
00:25:15,120 --> 00:25:21,600
 Now meanwhile, while this is going on, we have the condler cartilage, which develops

284
00:25:21,600 --> 00:25:31,120
 independently and later fuses with the developing mandibular ramus at about four months in utero.

285
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:35,440
 So here we have the condler cartilage.

286
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:40,840
 And here is the part that we were looking at right over here.

287
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:49,240
 So although the cone-shaped cartilage is replaced by bone eventually, its upper end will persist

288
00:25:49,240 --> 00:25:52,400
 as a site for growth.

289
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:58,400
 That condler cartilage we will see in the next slide.

290
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:06,960
 So that mandibular condler cartilage proliferates and is transformed into bone as the mandible

291
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:13,040
 grows downward and forward, again away from the cranial base.

292
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:19,920
 So both jaws are being moved downward and forward, but by totally different modes or

293
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:20,920
 mechanisms.

294
00:26:20,920 --> 00:26:27,520
 And this creates a gap between the two jaws into which the teeth can erupt.

295
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:33,680
 Now in addition to this endochondral ossification occurring at the condler cartilage, we're

296
00:26:33,680 --> 00:26:40,360
 also getting surface opposition, which occurs along the posterior surface of the ramus to

297
00:26:40,360 --> 00:26:44,440
 try to match and keep up with what the condyle is doing.

298
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:53,400
 You also get some opposition at the coronoid process as well as a little bit at the chin.

299
00:26:53,400 --> 00:26:59,040
 Surface resorption occurs along the anterior surface of the ramus, which makes room for

300
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:05,720
 the second and the third molars to erupt, which is opposite with what was happening at

301
00:27:05,720 --> 00:27:12,680
 the maxilla where we had a position to give room for the second and third molars.

302
00:27:12,680 --> 00:27:18,360
 Whereas here we have to take bone away in order to free up a row for those second and

303
00:27:18,360 --> 00:27:21,800
 third molars to erupt.

304
00:27:21,800 --> 00:27:30,440
 Now just like the maxilla, you also get alveolar bone growth vertically as the teeth erupt

305
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:33,880
 into position.

306
00:27:33,880 --> 00:27:42,200
 So we're getting in the mandible a mix of endochondral ossification and intermembranous

307
00:27:42,200 --> 00:27:44,120
 ossification.

308
00:27:44,120 --> 00:27:52,400
 Now as the mandible grows, it can stay at the same angle or it can tend to rotate because

309
00:27:52,400 --> 00:27:55,280
 it has this axis at the condyle.

310
00:27:55,280 --> 00:28:03,840
 So it can either rotate open in this direction or closed in this direction.

311
00:28:03,840 --> 00:28:10,360
 So an average growth rotation is where the condler growth roughly is equal to the amount

312
00:28:10,360 --> 00:28:12,320
 of molar eruption.

313
00:28:12,320 --> 00:28:19,300
 So the mandible truly just grows downward and forward at roughly the same orientation as

314
00:28:19,300 --> 00:28:21,760
 you can see here.

315
00:28:21,760 --> 00:28:30,080
 Now if we have a closing rotation or counterclockwise growth, that means that the growth of the

316
00:28:30,080 --> 00:28:38,060
 condyle is greatly exceeding the amount of molar eruption that we're seeing.

317
00:28:38,060 --> 00:28:47,400
 So that would lead to a shorter, lower anterior face height and a skeletal deep bite tendency.

318
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:52,800
 The opposite is opening rotation or clockwise growth.

319
00:28:52,800 --> 00:28:59,600
 This is where the condyl or growth is less than the amount of molar eruption leading to

320
00:28:59,600 --> 00:29:08,640
 a long anterior face height and skeletal anterior open bite tendency.

321
00:29:08,640 --> 00:29:12,360
 So here's a summary of everything that we talked about.

322
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:20,600
 The cranial vault, and we'll kind of focus mostly on this row here, mesenchymal is synonymous

323
00:29:20,600 --> 00:29:22,480
 with intermembranous.

324
00:29:22,480 --> 00:29:27,840
 So we're seeing intermembranous growth at the sutures in the surfaces, because there's

325
00:29:27,840 --> 00:29:31,080
 no cartilage in the cranial vault.

326
00:29:31,080 --> 00:29:35,520
 The cranial base, we see endocondral growth at the synchondroses.

327
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:41,800
 We also have a nominal amount of intermembranous growth that happens laterally to make the cranial

328
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:45,600
 base grow wider, but don't worry too much about that part.

329
00:29:45,600 --> 00:29:52,200
 The maxilla has purely intermembranous growth that's at both sutures and the surfaces directly

330
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:54,920
 – again, no cartilage here.

331
00:29:54,920 --> 00:30:01,800
 And the mandible, we mostly get intermembranous growth at its surfaces, but we're also getting

332
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:06,880
 endocondral growth at that condyle, which is pushing that mandible downward and forward,

333
00:30:06,880 --> 00:30:11,200
 which is a very important growth mechanism.

334
00:30:11,200 --> 00:30:16,240
 And of course, there are no sutures in the mandible, so that intermembranous growth is

335
00:30:16,240 --> 00:30:20,120
 purely at the surfaces.

336
00:30:20,120 --> 00:30:24,420
 The last high yield fact that I want to cover is a general trend.

337
00:30:24,420 --> 00:30:28,760
 Now we talked about all the different regions of the cranium in the face, and each of those

338
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:32,880
 skull bones exists in three planes of space.

339
00:30:32,880 --> 00:30:39,160
 There's width, length, and height, or more commonly referred to as transverse, anterior

340
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:42,640
 posterior, or AP, and vertical.

341
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:45,600
 And these will come up all throughout the series.

342
00:30:45,600 --> 00:30:53,760
 But generally, the transverse dimension stops growing first at around age 10 to 12, then

343
00:30:53,760 --> 00:31:03,000
 AP at 14 to 16, and finally vertical at 18 to 20, or even further on into later adult

344
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:04,000
 life.

345
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:09,720
 There are exceptions to this, but this is the general overarching trend that drives many

346
00:31:09,720 --> 00:31:15,560
 treatment decisions that we'll cover later in the series.

347
00:31:15,560 --> 00:31:20,280
 So that's it for this video, thank you so much for watching, please like this video if

348
00:31:20,280 --> 00:31:25,640
 you enjoyed it, and consider subscribing to this channel for more on dentistry.

349
